  <h1>Common Brain Injury Claim Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Fifteen common misconceptions that come up repeatedly in brain injury case intake. Each is addressed briefly with the underlying reality.</p>

  <h2>1. "Mild TBI is minor; I should just rest and move on."</h2>

  <p>The clinical label "mild" refers to acute severity (GCS 13&ndash;15, brief or no LOC) and does not mean the long-term impact will be minor. A meaningful minority of mild TBI patients develop persistent post-concussive syndrome with months-to-years duration. A mild TBI in a high-cognitive-demand occupation can end a career. Mild TBI cases regularly produce settlements of $100K&ndash;$500K, and clear-impact cases (documented neuropsychological deficits, vocational loss) reach higher. Take mild TBI seriously, get appropriate evaluation, and do not dismiss persistent symptoms as "just being tired."</p>

  <h2>2. "If the CT was normal, the brain injury wasn't real."</h2>

  <p>This is the single most consequential misconception. Standard CT is excellent for acute hemorrhage and gross structural injury, but it is insensitive to diffuse axonal injury, small contusions, and many other TBI patterns. Most mild and many moderate TBIs have normal or near-normal CT. MRI with appropriate sequences (susceptibility-weighted imaging, DTI where available) reveals injury invisible on CT. Neuropsychological testing demonstrates functional deficits even when imaging is negative. A normal CT rules out emergency surgical pathology; it does not rule out brain injury.</p>

  <h2>3. "Post-concussive syndrome is not a real diagnosis."</h2>

  <p>Post-concussive syndrome has been the subject of substantial peer-reviewed research and is recognized by major neurology, neurosurgery, and rehabilitation organizations. The clinical reality is that 15&ndash;30% of mild TBI patients develop symptoms lasting beyond the expected recovery window, with a subset experiencing months-to-years of impairment. Defense experts sometimes argue against PCS in litigation, but the underlying science is well-established. Documentation with validated symptom inventories, neuropsychological testing, and vestibular/oculomotor evaluation supports PCS claims effectively.</p>

  <h2>4. "Brain injury settlements happen fast."</h2>

  <p>Moderate-to-severe TBI cases typically take 1&ndash;3 years to resolve by settlement, and longer if they go to verdict. The reason: the medical situation needs to stabilize enough to project long-term needs accurately. Settling at six months for a severe TBI patient typically undervalues the case because the long-term prognosis is not yet clear. Mild TBI cases can resolve faster (6&ndash;18 months) because the medical course is shorter. Patience usually produces a better outcome.</p>

  <h2>5. "The attorney's contingency fee makes settlement less attractive than DIY."</h2>

  <p>Catastrophic injury cases are not viable for self-representation. The case requires medical record analysis, life-care planning, vocational rehabilitation expert work, economic loss analysis, and (often) multi-defendant litigation. The contingency fee (typically 33&ndash;40%) is the cost of having a competent attorney pursue the case at their own risk and cost. Self-represented TBI plaintiffs almost always recover dramatically less than represented plaintiffs &mdash; the net to the family is higher with an attorney even after the contingency fee.</p>

  <h2>6. "The insurance company will be fair."</h2>

  <p>Insurance carriers are obligated to act in good faith, but their commercial interest is in paying as little as possible. Early offers in catastrophic injury cases are nearly always well below the actual case value. The insurance adjuster's job is to manage the claim cost; they are not the patient's advocate. Treating the insurance carrier as adversarial (without being hostile) is the appropriate posture.</p>

  <h2>7. "If I have any fault, I can't recover."</h2>

  <p>Most states apply comparative negligence: recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's share of fault. In modified comparative states, recovery is barred only if the plaintiff is more than 50% (or 51%) at fault. Pure comparative negligence states (California, New York, Florida, several others) allow recovery reduced by the plaintiff's share even at high fault percentages. A few states still apply traditional contributory negligence (Alabama, Maryland, North Carolina, Virginia, DC). Comparative fault analysis is fact-intensive and should not be assumed without legal evaluation.</p>

  <h2>8. "If the at-fault party has no money, there's no case."</h2>

  <p>Often wrong. Recovery sources beyond the at-fault party's personal assets include: the at-fault party's insurance, the victim's underinsured motorist coverage, umbrella policies, third-party claims (employer, vehicle manufacturer, dram shop, premises owner), and (in some cases) state victim compensation funds. A good catastrophic injury attorney maps all available recovery sources before concluding a case is not viable.</p>

  <h2>9. "Kids recover better than adults from brain injury."</h2>

  <p>Partly true and partly false. Younger brains have greater plasticity, but very young brains are also more vulnerable to disrupted development &mdash; the same injury that an adult would recover from substantially can permanently alter the developmental trajectory of a young child. Pediatric TBI outcomes are highly age- and injury-specific. The optimistic stereotype that "children just bounce back" is not accurate for moderate-to-severe pediatric TBI.</p>

  <h2>10. "Helmet use prevents brain injury."</h2>

  <p>Helmets dramatically reduce the risk of skull fracture and severe focal brain injury, but they do not prevent all TBI. Helmets are designed for impact attenuation; they are less effective against rotational acceleration, which is the dominant mechanism of diffuse axonal injury. Concussions occur regularly in helmeted activities. In litigation, helmet status matters but does not eliminate the brain injury or the claim.</p>

  <h2>11. "Second impact syndrome is a sports-medicine fiction."</h2>

  <p>Second impact syndrome is rare but real. When an athlete sustains a second concussion before recovering from a first, the result can be diffuse cerebral swelling with a high mortality rate. The condition is most prominent in pediatric and adolescent athletes. Return-to-play protocols are designed specifically to prevent it. Mismanagement of return-to-play, particularly in school athletics, is a real litigation issue.</p>

  <h2>12. "Brain injury settlements are taxable income."</h2>

  <p>Compensatory damages on account of physical injury are not generally subject to federal income tax under IRC &sect; 104(a)(2). Brain injury is clearly a physical injury, so the bulk of a TBI settlement is non-taxable. Exceptions include punitive damages (generally taxable), interest awarded on the judgment (taxable), and certain emotional distress damages not tied to physical injury (potentially taxable). Settlement structure matters; consult a tax professional.</p>

  <h2>13. "A settlement will disqualify the patient from Medicaid and SSI."</h2>

  <p>Without planning, yes. With proper planning, no. A first-party special needs trust (Medicaid payback trust) holds settlement proceeds for the benefit of a disabled person without disqualifying them from Medicaid and SSI. The trust pays for supplemental needs (rehabilitation beyond Medicaid, equipment, family support, recreation, education) and at the beneficiary's death, the state Medicaid agency is repaid for benefits provided. Setting up the SNT before settlement closes is critical; doing it afterward is more complicated and sometimes impossible.</p>

  <h2>14. "Repeated concussions don't add up &mdash; each one is separate."</h2>

  <p>Repeated concussions have cumulative effects, particularly when the second occurs before recovery from the first. The literature on repeated subconcussive impacts (football, hockey, soccer) demonstrates cumulative damage that can manifest as chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) years after the exposure. Cumulative-trauma claims are well-established in major sports concussion settlements and are increasingly recognized in occupational contexts (military service, professional fighting).</p>

  <h2>15. "Once a settlement is signed, the family is done with the case."</h2>

  <p>Mostly true, but with important exceptions. Subrogation interests (health insurance, Medicare, Medicaid) need to be resolved out of the settlement. Special needs trust administration is ongoing. Structured settlement payments continue over time and have their own administrative considerations. Tax filings (for the taxable portions) need attention. For catastrophic TBI patients with significant settlements, the post-settlement administration is a substantial ongoing project, typically managed by a combination of attorney, trustee, and financial advisor.</p>

